/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_SharedInfrastructure Shared Build Infrastructure
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows
 * @brief INFRA-001 design for reusable build infrastructure across Foundation and future projects.
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructureStatus Status and Decision
 *
 * | Field | Value |
 * | ----- | ----- |
 * | Backlog task | `INFRA-001` |
 * | Design status | `IN PROGRESS` - phases 0 through 2 are complete; phase 3 is next |
 * | Shared repository | Publicly readable, restrictively licensed [ChecheRomo96/RoModularBuild](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/RoModularBuild), commit `494a304`, tag `v0.2.3` |
 * | Initial integration | `tools/RoModularBuild` version-pinned Git submodule |
 * | Current implementation owner | RoModularBuild for toolchains and hidden preset bases; Foundation for public adapters and project policy |
 * | Migration rule | No workflow behavior or public command changes during extraction |
 *
 * Foundation has now exercised the same configure, build, test, install,
 * export, package, and CI concepts across desktop, AVR, generic Arm, Arduino,
 * and an exact STM32 firmware consumer. That is enough evidence to identify a
 * reusable layer. It is not yet permission to move every build-related file.
 * Project policy and acceptance tests must remain with the project that owns
 * them.
 *
 * The selected direction is a small, separately versioned build-infrastructure
 * repository named `RoModularBuild`. Foundation and future projects such as MCC
 * will pin an exact infrastructure revision and retain thin repository-local
 * adapters. An optional `romodular` command-line frontend may be added later;
 * the initial extraction must continue to work through the existing `.sh` and
 * `.ps1` entrypoints.
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructureGoals Goals and Non-goals
 *
 * Goals:
 *
 * - define native and cross-compilation profiles once;
 * - keep Bash and PowerShell workflow behavior equivalent;
 * - give every repository reproducible, version-pinned infrastructure;
 * - preserve `build/<preset>` and `dist/<preset>` conventions;
 * - keep presets as the source of truth for compiler, architecture, and ABI;
 * - keep Debug and Release as configurations, not separate preset identities;
 * - allow project-specific feature options and package targets without teaching
 *   the shared layer about Foundation or MCC; and
 * - make upgrades explicit, reviewable, and independently reversible.
 *
 * Non-goals:
 *
 * - turning CMake into a hidden secondary build system;
 * - putting Foundation modules, tests, package metadata, or release policy in a
 *   shared repository;
 * - defining startup code, linker scripts, HAL/CMSIS, flashing, or board wiring
 *   for every microcontroller;
 * - generating one precompiled Foundation package per Arm part number;
 * - requiring Docker for native Windows or macOS validation; or
 * - creating the final `romodular` CLI before the underlying contract is stable.
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructureArchitecture Target Architecture
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph FoundationSharedInfrastructure {
 *     rankdir=TB;
 *     graph [bgcolor="transparent", pad="0.2", nodesep="0.45", ranksep="0.55"];
 *     node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10];
 *
 *     shared [label="RoModularBuild\nversioned workflow engine\ntoolchains + preset bases",
 *             fillcolor="#d6eaf8"];
 *     adapter [label="Repository adapter\nproject name + cache options\ntarget names + policy",
 *              fillcolor="#fcf3cf"];
 *     project [label="Foundation / MCC\nCMake project + source\ntests + examples + docs",
 *              fillcolor="#d5f5e3"];
 *     firmware [label="Firmware target fixture\nstartup + linker + board\nflash + hardware evidence",
 *               fillcolor="#fadbd8"];
 *
 *     shared -> adapter [label="pinned version"];
 *     adapter -> project [label="stable local commands"];
 *     project -> firmware [label="installed package"];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * The shared repository must not depend on Foundation, MCC, or any other
 * consumer. Dependency direction is one-way: a project selects a pinned
 * infrastructure version and adds its own adapter and acceptance policy.
 *
 * Proposed shared layout:
 *
 * @code
 * RoModularBuild/
 * ├── CMakePresets.json
 * ├── cmake/
 * │   ├── presets/
 * │   │   ├── native.json
 * │   │   ├── arm-none-eabi.json
 * │   │   └── avr-gcc.json
 * │   └── toolchains/
 * │       ├── arm-none-eabi.cmake
 * │       ├── arm-none-eabi/
 * │       ├── avr-gcc.cmake
 * │       └── avr-gcc/
 * ├── scripts/
 * │   ├── common.sh
 * │   ├── common.ps1
 * │   ├── configure.sh / configure.ps1
 * │   ├── build.sh / build.ps1
 * │   ├── test.sh / test.ps1
 * │   ├── install.sh / install.ps1
 * │   └── clean.sh / clean.ps1
 * ├── actions/
 * └── docs/
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructureOwnership Ownership Boundary
 *
 * | Current asset | Target owner | Rationale |
 * | ------------- | ------------ | --------- |
 * | Generic Arm and AVR toolchain mechanics | `RoModularBuild` | Compiler discovery, CPU/ABI flags, sysroots, and find-root behavior are not Foundation-specific. |
 * | Native compiler/generator preset bases | `RoModularBuild` | macOS, Linux, and Windows host profiles are reusable compatibility identities. |
 * | `common`, configure, build, test, install, and clean workflow engine | `RoModularBuild` | Argument parsing, safe paths, CMake invocation, configuration selection, and non-zero-test policy are reusable. |
 * | Root `CMakePresets.json` and public project presets | Foundation | The project chooses which shared profiles it advertises and adds its cache defaults and artifact identity. |
 * | Foundation CMake options and module dependencies | Foundation | `FOUNDATION_*` feature selection is public project policy. |
 * | `FoundationConfig.cmake`, build identity, install/export targets | Foundation | These define the Foundation package contract. |
 * | Package identity and standalone package consumer | Foundation | They inspect Foundation headers, metadata, target names, and archive names. |
 * | Examples, Arduino, launchers, and output validation | Foundation | They validate Foundation APIs and presentation policy. |
 * | STM32G0B1 startup, linker script, firmware checks, and SWD evidence | Foundation target fixture, later a firmware repository if reused | An exact MCU memory map and execution procedure are not generic library-build infrastructure. |
 * | Repository workflow entrypoints and release publication | Foundation | Permissions, artifacts, license gates, release names, and acceptance evidence remain repository policy. |
 * | Reusable CI job implementation | `RoModularBuild`, after scripts stabilize | Shared jobs may call project adapters, but each repository retains a small pinned caller workflow. |
 *
 * `export.sh`, `validate-package`, reusable CI, and release automation must not
 * move in the first extraction. Their current implementation contains explicit
 * Foundation option names, targets, package paths, examples, and policy. They
 * can be split only after the adapter contract is proven.
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructurePresetContract Preset and Toolchain Contract
 *
 * The shared layer provides hidden or clearly prefixed base presets. A project
 * publishes its own preset by inheriting a shared base and adding local cache
 * values. For example, the shared layer may define a Cortex-M0+/soft-float base,
 * while Foundation continues to publish `arm_none_eabi_cortex_m0plus_soft` and
 * records `FOUNDATION_PLATFORM` locally.
 *
 * Shared cache variables use the `ROMODULAR_` prefix. Project feature options
 * retain their project prefix. The migration must temporarily accept the
 * existing Foundation toolchain variables so saved commands do not break; the
 * shared names become canonical only after both paths produce identical flags.
 *
 * Package boundaries remain based on binary compatibility:
 *
 * - native packages: operating system, compiler/toolchain, and architecture;
 * - generic Arm packages: instruction-set core, float ABI, FPU, compiler/runtime,
 *   and relevant flags;
 * - AVR packages: the exact MCU may remain necessary because `-mmcu` controls
 *   device-specific code generation and runtime selection; and
 * - firmware targets: exact MCU/board memory map, startup, device SDK, and
 *   programming procedure.
 *
 * Therefore, `STM32G0B1CBT6` is an exact Foundation acceptance target, while
 * the reusable library compatibility profile is Cortex-M0+/soft-float. The
 * shared repository must not grow one generic Arm library preset per MCU.
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructureDistribution Distribution and Pinning
 *
 * The first integration uses a Git submodule at a stable relative path such as
 * `tools/RoModularBuild`. Each project pins an exact commit belonging to a
 * tagged infrastructure release. This solves the bootstrap constraint: CMake
 * parses presets and loads a toolchain before the project can use
 * `FetchContent`, so the required files must already exist before configure.
 *
 * Required operational rules:
 *
 * - clone with submodules or run the documented initialization command;
 * - CI checkout enables submodules explicitly;
 * - upgrades change one recorded submodule commit and run the complete project
 *   matrix before merge;
 * - no branch-floating reference such as `main` is accepted by a consumer;
 * - `RoModularBuild` follows an independent semantic version;
 * - `0.x` releases may evolve the adapter contract; `1.x` freezes it; and
 * - public visibility provides reproducible read access but grants no permission
 *   to use, modify, or redistribute the code; the restrictive license remains
 *   in force until separate legal review authorizes another model.
 *
 * A future `romodular` CLI may install or update the pinned infrastructure and
 * expose commands such as `romodular build <preset>`. It must remain a frontend
 * over the same CMake presets and workflow contract, not become another source
 * of compiler or target truth.
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructureMigration Migration Plan
 *
 * | Phase | State | Deliverable | Exit evidence |
 * | ----- | ----- | ----------- | ------------- |
 * | 0. Design and inventory | `DONE` | Ownership boundary, target layout, pinning strategy, compatibility rules, and migration gates recorded on this page | Warning-as-error Doxygen build and reviewed Foundation inventory |
 * | 1. Bootstrap repository | `DONE` | Publicly readable, restrictively licensed repository `RoModularBuild` provides license guardrails, independent versioning, documentation, hidden preset namespaces, Bash/PowerShell validators, CTest contract tests, and a three-platform validation workflow | Exact tag `v0.1.1` passed Linux Bash, macOS Bash, and Windows PowerShell in [run 36459142788](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/RoModularBuild/actions/runs/36459142788) |
 * | 2. Extract toolchains and preset bases | `DONE` | Foundation pins RoModularBuild `v0.2.3`; hidden shared bases own reusable native/Arm/AVR selections, while thin local adapters preserve public presets and `FOUNDATION_*` variables | Local macOS Arm64, Cortex-M0+/soft, and AVR5 `compile_commands.json` files are byte-identical before/after migration; AVR Release archive SHA-256 remains `50f5a152a3d14eb9cbdb1ab3280e638078251ad5358968242aae8270943a3aaf`; macOS passes 162/162 Debug tests; macOS and AVR Release exports pass package-identity validation; and direct `ROMODULAR_*` configuration and 1.x precedence behavior pass. RoModularBuild exact-tag [run 36480576076](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/RoModularBuild/actions/runs/36480576076) passes Linux, macOS, and Windows. On integration commit `73bc2df`, Foundation [native run 36481996365](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36481996365), [embedded run 36481996289](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36481996289), and [documentation run 36481996372](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36481996372) all pass after recursively checking out the public submodule without secrets. |
 * | 3. Extract workflow engine | `TODO` | Move generic lifecycle logic; preserve current Foundation commands through thin wrappers | Native and embedded CI pass with Bash/PowerShell parity and clean/fresh guarantees |
 * | 4. Extract reusable CI | `TODO` | Provide pinned reusable jobs or actions; keep repository permissions and release policy local | Foundation callers pass native, embedded, docs, sanitizer, coverage, and failure-evidence gates |
 * | 5. Adopt from a second project | `TODO` | Consume the same tagged infrastructure from MCC without copying implementation | Both projects pin one release and pass their independent package-consumer tests |
 * | 6. Consider the CLI | `DEFERRED` | Evaluate `romodular configure/build/test/export` after two repositories prove the contract | CLI adds no target truth and preserves direct script/CMake operation |
 *
 * Each phase is a separate reviewable commit or pull request. Foundation must
 * stay buildable at every boundary. Do not delete the local implementation
 * until the replacement has passed the same native and embedded matrices.
 *
 * @section FoundationSharedInfrastructureAcceptance INFRA-001 Acceptance
 *
 * `INFRA-001` is complete only when:
 *
 * - the shared repository exists and has an independently tagged version;
 * - Foundation pins that exact version without floating branches;
 * - generic files contain no `Foundation`, `MCC`, or project-specific target
 *   names;
 * - Foundation's current commands and output directories remain compatible;
 * - Bash and PowerShell paths pass the hosted native matrix;
 * - all advertised embedded packages retain their validated architecture/ABI;
 * - package, example, Arduino, STM32, release, and legal policy remain local;
 * - a second repository consumes the same release successfully; and
 * - rollback consists only of restoring the previous pinned revision.
 *
 * Until those gates pass, the checked-in Foundation implementation remains the
 * authoritative and supported workflow.
 */
